content.text:
I see the issue. The algorithm scans from the **last** grid point backward (`i = Pn - 1` down to 0) to find the first alive candidate with a non-empty `S`, and that becomes the output point. But if all candidates are dead, it still uses whatever was in `buffer[0]` and `buffer[1]` from the previous iteration.

Looking at line 229-234:

```206:234:simplify.cpp
        for (int i = 0; i < Pn; ++i) {
            webtrace::Candidate cand;
            cand.grid_pt_idx = i;
            if (dead[i]) {
                cand.alive = false;
                step.candidates.push_back(std::move(cand));
                continue;
            }
            find_F(P[i], S[i], F[i]);
            cand.F = F[i];
            if (!intersect(F[i], Gi, new_S[i])) {
                dead[i] = true;
                dead_cnt++;
                cand.alive = false;
            } else {
                cand.alive = true;
                cand.S = new_S[i];
            }
            step.candidates.push_back(std::move(cand));
        }

        bool has_candidate = false;
        for (int i = Pn - 1; i >= 0 && !has_candidate; --i) {
            if (dead[i] || new_S[i].empty()) continue;
            buffer[0] = P[i];
            buffer[1] = new_S[i].front();
            has_candidate = true;
        }
```

If all candidates are dead or have empty `new_S`, `has_candidate` remains false and `buffer` isn't updated. The loop then breaks at line 238 (`if (!has_candidate || dead_cnt == Pn) break;`), but lines 243-244 still output whatever is in `buffer[0]` and `buffer[1]`.

**The bug:** when all candidates die, the algorithm should output the last known alive candidate before they all died, but if `buffer` was never set (e.g., first segment and all die immediately), it could contain garbage or a dead point.

For Sample Trace 3, if all grid points die in the first step, `buffer` might still contain a dead grid point from initialization. The fix would be to ensure `buffer` is always initialized to a valid alive candidate before the loop starts, or to not output anything when `has_candidate` is false.

